{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Guard</b>. Most of the commentators interpreted 'your foot' as in 'did not make his feet' [i.e., go to worship idols], but its plain sense is: since he mentioned the king, and that those near him do not rejoice because they fear him — so one who comes to his palace must be careful in his attire and in the words of his mouth. He said: beyond this, you must be careful when you go to the house of Elohim, and the meaning of 'guard your foot' is like a person who says: such-and-such a place is dangerous, and if you do not know to be careful — guard your foot." "<b>And the meaning of 'and near to hear'.</b> Although I said to you 'guard your foot,' Elohim is not distant like a king of flesh and blood; rather, He is nearer to receive your words if you call upon Him in truth." "<b>Sacrifice</b>. That the fools would offer it, for they know only to do evil, like 'there is no good in man' to eat and drink, and the meaning is that they have no proper knowledge to do good. Some say that the fools do not know even to do evil, which is close to the inclination of the human heart — all the more so the good. And some say the meaning of 'evil' is like 'will.'"], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The phrase לא עשה רגליו alludes to Proverbs 4:27 and related rabbinic idiom for straying after idolatry; rendered literally without expanding the reference."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:40.676124175Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}